<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capitalism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Capitalism is an economic system based on private ownership of the means of production, production for profit and the accumulation of capital through competitive markets and wage labour.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic of Genoa built a commercial empire from the Black Sea to the Atlantic, pioneered the contracts, insurance and public debt of European <b>capitalism</b>, and through its bankers financed the Spanish Empire, making a small Ligurian city one of the makers of the early modern world economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Capitalism</b> and Slavery of 1944 the Trinidadian historian Eric Williams, later his country’s first prime minister, argued that Britain abolished the trade and slavery when the West Indian sugar economy was declining and no longer served British capitalism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles Darwin’s On the Origin of Species (1859) undermined religious certainty and the idea of human uniqueness, and Karl Marx’s Das Kapital (1867) argued that <b>capitalism</b> was full of contradictions.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Pan-Africanists also differed over whether the movement was defined by race, uniting Black people everywhere, or by the continent, including the Arab states of North Africa; over <b>capitalism</b> and socialism; and over the place of the diaspora in African politics.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Max Weber’s essay The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of <b>Capitalism</b> (1904–1905) linked Protestant, especially Calvinist, discipline to the rise of capitalism, a thesis still debated and widely criticised.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmed Rıza wanted a centralized, Turkish-dominated empire free of European influence; Prince Sabahaddin a decentralized, federal state built on <b>capitalism</b> and liberalism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
